## Salvaged artifact — from a duplicate dispatch that will NOT ship as its own PR

⛔ **Not a competing implementation.** This seat was dispatched on #10826 in parallel with the work that became **PR #11208**, which is the implementation that lands. The local commit this rig came from is being discarded and its worktree torn down; nothing here will be pushed, and no PR will be opened from it. Posting only the measurement harness, because it is the hard part of **item 2** above and whoever picks that card up should not have to rebuild it.

⚠️ **Written against pre-#11208 source — it needs rebasing onto the landed `getMany` path.** It was authored on a branch cut from `21756b325`, before #11208 existed. Nothing in it is exotic, but expect at least these adjustments: the `EXPECTED`/`VALUES` constants and `makeCountingQl` helper already exist in `resolve-authz-context.test.ts`; and the `BEFORE` case below only runs at all because the landed implementation keeps a per-key fallback for occupants without `getMany` — verify that is still true before trusting it as a before-state.

### How the leg count works

The one idea worth keeping. `loadRows` stands in for the real service's namespace row load, so **one call is one `sys_setting` query**. A **leg** is a row load that *starts while nothing else is in flight* — i.e. the number of sequential round-trip waves. Three loads issued inside one `Promise.all` all increment `inFlight` synchronously before any of them resumes past its first `await`, so they count as **one** leg; three awaited in sequence count as three. That is the leg definition cloud#1539 used, and it is what makes "3 queries, 1 leg" measurable rather than asserted.

```ts
  /**
   * A settings-service double that MEASURES what the resolver asks for.
   *
   *  - `queries` — every row load.
   *  - `legs`    — every row load that STARTS while nothing else is in
   *                flight, i.e. the number of sequential round-trip waves.
   *
   * `batched: false` reproduces the pre-#10826 occupant (only `get`), which is
   * also the shape the resolver still falls back to for a host-provided
   * settings service that predates `getMany`.
   */
  function makeSettingsRig(
    values: Record[string, unknown],
    { batched }: { batched: boolean },
  ) {
    const stats = { queries: 0, legs: 0 };
    const calls: Array[{ method: string; args: any[] }] = [];
    let inFlight = 0;
    const loadRows = async () => {
      stats.queries += 1;
      if (inFlight === 0) stats.legs += 1;   // nothing else in flight -> a new wave
      inFlight += 1;
      try {
        await Promise.resolve();
        return values;
      } finally {
        inFlight -= 1;
      }
    };
    const pick = (loaded: Record[string, unknown], key: string) =>
      key in loaded ? { value: loaded[key], source: 'tenant' } : { value: undefined, source: 'default' };
    const rig: any = {
      stats,
      calls,
      async get(namespace: string, key: string, ctx: any) {
        calls.push({ method: 'get', args: [namespace, key, ctx] });
        return pick(await loadRows(), key);
      },
    };
    if (batched) {
      // NOTE the THIRD parameter — this is also the fix for item 3 above:
      // the double must accept `ctx` to be able to assert it was forwarded.
      rig.getMany = async (namespace: string, keys: readonly string[], ctx: any) => {
        calls.push({ method: 'getMany', args: [namespace, [...keys], ctx] });
        const loaded = await loadRows();
        const out: Record[string, unknown] = {};
        for (const key of keys) out[key] = pick(loaded, key);
        return out;
      };
    }
    return rig;
  }

  const VALUES = { timezone: 'Asia/Tokyo', locale: 'ja-JP', currency: 'JPY' };
  const EXPECTED = { timezone: 'Asia/Tokyo', locale: 'ja-JP', currency: 'JPY' };

  it('BEFORE — a per-key occupant issues three namespace reads in one leg', async () => {
    const settings = makeSettingsRig(VALUES, { batched: false });
    const ql = makeCountingQl({ sys_setting: [] });
    const loc = await resolveLocalizationContext({ ql, settings, tenantId: 'o1', userId: 'u1' });
    expect(loc).toEqual(EXPECTED);
    expect(settings.stats).toEqual({ queries: 3, legs: 1 });
    // The settings path answered, so the direct `sys_setting` fallback is not
    // reached — the three reads above are the whole cost.
    expect(ql.counts.sys_setting ?? 0).toBe(0);
  });

  it('AFTER — the batched occupant issues ONE namespace read, in the same one leg', async () => {
    const settings = makeSettingsRig(VALUES, { batched: true });
    const ql = makeCountingQl({ sys_setting: [] });
    const loc = await resolveLocalizationContext({ ql, settings, tenantId: 'o1', userId: 'u1' });
    expect(loc).toEqual(EXPECTED);
    expect(settings.stats).toEqual({ queries: 1, legs: 1 });
    expect(ql.counts.sys_setting ?? 0).toBe(0);
  });

  // Item 3: the context assertion the current pins structurally cannot make.
  it('asks for all three keys of the one namespace in a single call, with the same context', async () => {
    const settings = makeSettingsRig(VALUES, { batched: true });
    await resolveLocalizationContext({
      ql: makeCountingQl({ sys_setting: [] }), settings, tenantId: 'o1', userId: 'u1',
    });
    expect(settings.calls).toEqual([
      { method: 'getMany', args: ['localization', ['timezone', 'locale', 'currency'], { tenantId: 'o1', userId: 'u1' }] },
    ]);
  });
```

⚠️ **Typing note:** the generic type arguments above are written with **square** brackets (`Record[string, unknown]`, `Array[{ … }]`) instead of angle brackets, because this repo has measured GitHub's body sanitizer eating short angle-bracket fragments even inside fenced code. Restore them to angle brackets when pasting into the test file.

### Reverse-verified, not merely written

Run against the pre-fix source with the tests kept in place, the `AFTER` case failed with exactly the before-state numbers:

```text
AssertionError: expected { queries: 3, legs: 1 } to deeply equal { queries: 1, legs: 1 }
```

That single line is the card's whole calibration as a measurement: **queries 3 → 1, legs 1 → 1**. It confirms cloud#1539's finding independently — the three reads really did already run in one leg — which is why #10826 was correctly scheduled as a query-count fix and not a latency fix. A future regression to a sequential loop would move the `legs` half of that assertion while leaving every call-count pin green, which is the gap item 2 exists to close.

